# The places Polish speaks at that Italian does not, written for Polish rather
# than lifted for Italian. A locus rule says where the formants go; the rule
# for a phoneme sets its source and then calls one of these.
#
# The numbers are a starting point and the ear settles them. What they are
# read off is Italian's own: its palatal locus is f2 1800 and f3 2400, its
# dentals 1700 and 2600, and a labial's low second formant 850 -- so the
# language's own numbers say what scale these are on.

# Polish sz, ż, cz and dż are retroflex, articulated further back than the
# palato-alveolars Italian spells with sc and gi. The signature of a retroflex
# is a low third formant, so f3 comes down from Italian's 2400 to 2200 and f2
# from 1800 to 1700. Nothing here touches the first formant: the rule for the
# phoneme sets that before it asks for a place.
rule pol_retroflex_Fv takes 1 from is_val.obj
  call ZZfence_null
  set f2_in to 1700
  set f2_out to 1700
  set f3_in to 2200
  set f3_out to 2200
  match
end

# And the alveolo-palatals -- ś, ź, ć, dź -- which are further forward than
# either, with a third formant higher than the palatal's rather than lower.
# Nothing calls this yet: the letters that want it are still spoken as the
# retroflexes, because a distinct sound needs a phoneme of its own and Polish
# has not been given one. What it is here for is that the number is written
# down where the next person can find it.
rule pol_alveolopalatal_Fv takes 1 from is_val.obj
  call ZZfence_null
  set f2_in to 2000
  set f2_out to 2000
  set f3_in to 2700
  set f3_out to 2700
  match
end
